WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.920
. Hey, here's a story that this is not like
a big news story, but people flagged it for

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:05.920
me.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:11.230
And it's just kind of, you know, anytime there's
a win in the battle of the churning of ideas,

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:14.610
that's the new hot phrase to churn ideas like
butter.

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:15.929
I do like to mention it.

00:00:15.929 --> 00:00:19.920
Eric Weinstein is a member of the Intellectual
Dark Web.

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:20.920
Nice guy.

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:22.460
I've spoken to him a few times.

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:28.289
He was interviewed by a guy named David Fuller,
who has a YouTube channel called Rebel Wisdom.

00:00:28.289 --> 00:00:29.820
I've been interviewed by David Fuller.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:34.320
Previously, they had a conversation a couple
of days ago, and David Fuller asked Eric Weinstein

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:35.790
an interesting question.

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:40.110
Are there any critiques that you think have
been valuable or useful now?

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:44.121
I can't actually tell whether the question
is, are there critiques of the intellectual

00:00:44.121 --> 00:00:49.730
dark web that have been useful or are there
critiques of the left by people on the left

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:50.890
that have been useful?

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:52.610
I'm not actually sure which.

00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:55.460
The question is asking.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:01:00.830
But what was interesting was that Eric Weinstein
said a lot of people are doing schoolyard

00:01:00.830 --> 00:01:02.190
type stuff.

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:07.370
But when David Pakman is at his best, which
clearly is not all the time, that's OK when

00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:08.440
I'm at my best.

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:10.000
I've done some good critiques.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:14.510
And again, I don't know if it's of the idee
w or of the left from the left.

00:01:14.510 --> 00:01:15.510
Let's take a look.

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:20.080
Are there any critiques that you think were
invaluable and useful?

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:21.080
Very few.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:30.320
There've been a few, but very few.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:37.390
Nine.

00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:45.070
It's really been pretty slim, Pickens David
Pakman at his best.

00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:52.590
I think has been a pretty astute and fair
observer on the harder you want to call it,

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:53.590
harder left me.

00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:59.170
He served sort of weirdly between the WOAK
and the Ivy W..

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:02.040
Are there people who are very smart?

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:07.110
Like, for example, I think of Sam Ceder as
being very informed on policy terms, like

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:11.730
he's actually following a particular bill
or, you know, probably knows the names of

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:17.090
staffers on the Hill, that kind of granular
detail.

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:21.739
The problem with him is that he's got a strong
trolling game and kind of a comedic ridicule

00:02:21.739 --> 00:02:24.030
game that is a turnoff to me.

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:25.030
OK.

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:26.030
As a wit.

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:28.500
But it's not OK as a main course.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:37.779
And so I think that you could pick through
the Sam Cedar stuff and find reasonable stuff,

00:02:37.779 --> 00:02:42.010
but I wouldn't recommend that because of the
reliance on ridicule.

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:46.579
So there is Eric Weinstein saying, I think
if I understand it correctly, that when I'm

00:02:46.579 --> 00:02:50.760
at my best, which is not all the time, of
course I've done some okay critiques either

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:55.849
of the intellectual dark web, or maybe he
means critiques of the left from the left.

00:02:55.849 --> 00:02:57.359
I believe that this is a compliment.

00:02:57.359 --> 00:03:03.279
Now, if you've not been following the intellectual
dark web or Wolken s, he says I'm between

00:03:03.279 --> 00:03:06.120
the idee W and the WOAK left.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:10.439
These are two different movements that have
surfaced, at least starting online.

00:03:10.439 --> 00:03:15.450
The intellectual dark web is a sort of group
of commentators who could be on the left or

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:16.450
on the right.

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:19.150
But their big thing is they're opposed to
identity politics.

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:24.919
They're opposed to political correctness and
sacred cows, language policing, restrictions

00:03:24.919 --> 00:03:27.819
on speech taboos, etc.

00:03:27.819 --> 00:03:32.829
It includes people like Eric Weinstein himself,
as well as his brother, Brett.

00:03:32.829 --> 00:03:35.129
Includes Sam Harris and Michael Shermer.

00:03:35.129 --> 00:03:38.040
And then I don't know who else is within it
or outside of it.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:45.209
I'm not really sure the WOAK movement or WOAK
ness refers generally to the left wing movement

00:03:45.209 --> 00:03:51.430
that includes a particular type of cultural
awareness around race and feminism and LGBT

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:54.659
activism and socially liberal causes.

00:03:54.659 --> 00:04:00.139
Racial justice is a big thing and it's sometimes
used as a pejorative and sometimes it's not.

00:04:00.139 --> 00:04:01.139
Sometimes it's.

00:04:01.139 --> 00:04:03.010
Have you become WOAK as a positive thing?

00:04:03.010 --> 00:04:10.409
I think in this context, Eric is using weakness
as a pejorative and idee w as good and maybe

00:04:10.409 --> 00:04:12.329
as placing me between the two.

00:04:12.329 --> 00:04:18.289
So as soon as this happened, there were people
online furious that if Eric Weinstein is saying

00:04:18.289 --> 00:04:22.259
something good about David Pakman, David Pakman
must be really bad.

00:04:22.259 --> 00:04:27.190
Other people saw this and said, hey, that's
actually pretty interesting what I'm hearing.

00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:29.610
I'm leaving it at this.

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:35.690
It sounds to me like Eric is just saying I'm
at least honest and genuine in my critiques.

00:04:35.690 --> 00:04:40.410
I know that Eric has not agreed with every
one of my critiques of the idea w we've spoken

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:43.050
about it before and that's fine.

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:49.060
But for me this is less about, oh, where do
I agree or disagree with the woak left or

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:50.060
with the idea.

00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:57.500
W for me it's more about my commitment to
you as a viewer or listener has always been.

00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:03.129
I'm not going to just say stuff for clicks
when it's not really what I believe.

00:05:03.129 --> 00:05:08.080
If what I really believe generates interest
and builds an audience, that's great.

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:12.060
If it doesn't and you all know there have
been cases where I've just given you my opinion

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:16.009
and lost subscribers, well then that's too
bad.

00:05:16.009 --> 00:05:20.490
But my priority is I'll tell you things as
I see them.

00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:26.949
And hopefully me being genuine and honest
is what Eric is pointing to here.

00:05:26.949 --> 00:05:29.879
I've spoken to Eric about having him on the
program.

00:05:29.879 --> 00:05:31.990
Still an open invitation to do it anytime
he's ready.

